{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310639,"nonce":"Bb8h9em2Bewop2ECskidlLJe06A=","title":"Re-running nue/llama-chat without scrubbing .rayzor first crashes (SIGTRAP) at high rate. compare.sh and bench.sh hide this by always ...","message":"Three-probe discriminator on the SIGTRAP 133 surface (full results in bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption.md → \"DISCRIMINATOR RESULTS (2026-06-04)\"):\n\n- **Tier-promotion disabled** (`enable_tier_promotion=false` + `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry=false` in [tier]): **6/10 SIGTRAP unchanged** vs 6/10 baseline. The \"BLADE replays a stale LLVM tier-3 fn ptr\" framing this doc opens with is **REFUTED** — the bug exists at the Cranelift Baseline tier with all tier promotion off.\n- **`RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`**: no W0020, no new trap-stub entries beyond the 5 benign baseline (ArrayKeyValueIterator/ArrayIterator/TreeNode/BalancedTree new + the matching __reflect_ctor_wrap cascade). No silent dispatch failure being papered over.\n- **Prompt-content sensitivity**: 8/10 fail on 8-token sky prompt vs 2/10 fail on 9-token Paris prompt — content matters, not raw length, not warm-cache fn-ptr drift.\n\nThe confirmed hypothesis is **H-kvcache-edge** — first decode call at seq_pos=prompt_len trapping in a content-dependent way at the KVCache.append / GQAttention bmm boundary. See bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption.md \"DISCRIMINATOR RESULTS\" section for the full evidence chain and fix path.\n\nThis file is RETIRED as an independent investigation; warm-cache and cold-cache crash signatures converged (see \"POST-COMPILER-FIX STATUS\" below) and the discriminator confirms they're the same bug. The original a247060 / `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` framing is no longer correct.\n\n---\n\n\nAfter the cross-module-CallDirect cascade fix landed (stdlib_function_name_map renumber + qualified_name on forward-ref stubs + Path E strict assert; tracked in bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption.md), the cold-cache and warm-cache crash signatures CONVERGED:\n\n- Cold cache: 4/10 success, 6/10 SIGSEGV at `[lwt] 3` inside `metadataFromReader`.\n- Warm cache (previously deterministic SIGTRAP at JIT bringup): now matches the same shape — crash position is the same `metadataFromReader` block; trap-class changed from SIGTRAP to SIGSEGV.\n\nThis is **likely the same bug** as bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption's post-fix repro; the original \"BLADE restored stale LLVM tier-3 fn ptr\" framing no longer matches the evidence (this build is no-llvm-backend and the trap class is SIGSEGV not SIGTRAP). See that file's \"POST-COMPILER-FIX STATUS (2026-06-03)\" section for the carry-forward hypothesis ranking; this file is now a SYMPTOM/REPRO-TRACKER for the warm-cache variant.\n\nThe compare.sh / bench.sh cache-scrub workaround is still required for both variants (warm-cache success rate is currently 0/N because every warm replay path lands on the same crash), but the diagnosis is no longer \"BLADE replays a stale LLVM pointer\" — it is the same runtime UAF/dispatch hazard that hits cold-cache.\n\nRecommended next workflow: see bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption.md, \"POST-COMPILER-FIX STATUS\" section. Carry the lldb-attach + `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` discriminator from there.\n\n---\n\n\nRunning `target/release/rayzor run nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx -- \u003cgguf\u003e \u003cprompt\u003e \u003cn\u003e ...` a second time WITHOUT first scrubbing `.rayzor/` crashes with `Trace/BPT trap: 5` (SIGTRAP) at JIT bringup, before the first token is emitted.\n\nThe first run on a cold cache succeeds (or hits the unrelated ~50% bringup flake from bugs_bench_measures_wrong_thing). Subsequent warm-cache runs crash deterministically when the prior run actually completed and wrote the cache.\n\n\nBoth `tools/llama-diff/compare.sh` (line 95) and `tools/llama-diff/bench.sh` (in the run-once primitive) do `rm -rf .rayzor` before invoking rayzor. This sidesteps the bug entirely but means:\n- Every harness run pays cold-start JIT compile (~1-2s)\n- The Custom preset's `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry = true` config (shipped in a247060) is effectively single-shot — the LLVM tier-3 compile result never gets cached and reused\n\n\nThe interaction between BLADE cache replay and `upgrade_to_llvm()`. On the second run:\n\n1. BLADE cache loads — restores function pointers, metadata, vtable_init / module_init plumbing from the prior run\n2. Some of those pointers came from the LLVM tier-3 bulk compile (the `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` path in src/main.rs run_file)\n3. The new process's LLVM context is fresh — the prior process's compiled-code addresses are stale by definition\n4. The new run does NOT re-trigger upgrade_to_llvm because BLADE cache claims the relevant fns are already compiled (the `llvm_compiled` AtomicBool from compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs is restored via the cache)\n5. When execution reaches one of those fns, it jumps to a stale address → SIGTRAP\n\nThe flake when caches are clean (~50% rate, documented in bugs_bench_measures_wrong_thing) is a SEPARATE issue — that's a race in the LLVM bringup itself, not a cache-replay problem. Both need fixing but are orthogonal.\n\n\n1. Confirm by running with `RAYZOR_DISABLE_BLADE=1` (or whatever the existing toggle is) — if warm-cache runs succeed under that flag, BLADE cache replay is the culprit.\n2. Read `compile_all_with_llvm_mcjit` at compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs:2471 — note the \"already compiled\" early-return at line 2474. The check is against `self.llvm_compiled: Arc\u003cMutex\u003cbool\u003e\u003e`. If BLADE replay restores `true` here, the new run skips the actual MCJIT compile and assumes function pointers from the cache are valid — they aren't.\n3. The fix likely lives in tiered_backend.rs near the cache restore path: when restoring from BLADE, reset `llvm_compiled` to false so a fresh `upgrade_to_llvm()` can fire. OR: never cache function pointers that came from LLVM tier 3 — only cache up through tier 2 (Cranelift optimized).\n\n\nHigh — currently `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` is paying its compile cost on every run because BLADE is unsafe to use with it, so the headline reason for shipping `TierPreset::Custom` (amortise LLVM compile across runs) is unrealised. The Custom preset still works correctness-wise but its perf story leans entirely on warm OS page cache + a fast first-time LLVM compile, not on cross-run JIT caching.\n\n\n- [[bugs-bench-measures-wrong-thing]] — bench harness exposes the cold-cache JIT bringup flake at ~50% rate\n- [[bugs-probes-cranelift-only]] — describes how `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` was wired in a247060 and why it sometimes fails to fire at all\n- [[bugs-rayzor-cache-scrub]] — different cache-related crash mode; that one was about workflow intermediate states leaving stale wrapper symbols. This one is specifically about LLVM tier-3 pointer staleness across runs.\n- a247060 — the commit that introduced auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_llama_chat_cached_run_crash` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}